The dinner story scales when the guests start quoting each other. One person says, “I think the joke offended him.” By the fifth retelling, it’s “Everyone saw him get offended.” Now the next person walks in expecting hostility and reads an ordinary silence as further confirmation.

That’s the version of “mass psychosis” I hear you pointing at: a shared narrative changes how people interpret what happens next, and those interpretations feed back into the narrative. That loop can emerge through ordinary retelling. A viral clip and a hundred reactions to it can feel like a hundred witnesses.

For Pantheon, this gives in twos a sharper use. Put the version people are reacting to beside the earliest traceable account. Version control shows when “I think” became “everyone saw.” Provenance shows whether anyone actually brought a second observation. And if the story changed how people behaved afterward, record that as a new event. A story can have real consequences even when its original claim is still unverified.